    "date": "2024 Q2",
    "id": "sailfishos",
    "banner": "/jortdeboer_web/assets/res/projects/banner-sfos-fp4.png",
    "icon": "device-mobile",
    "title": "SailfishOS on Fairphone 4",
    "focus": "Mobile OS Porting & Open Source Contribution",
    "images": [],
    "short": "Developed a SailfishOS port for Fairphone 4.",
    "relProgLang": [
      "bash",
      "py",
      "c-cpp"
    ],
    "case": [
      {
        "id": "overview",
        "layout": "text-image",
        "title": "Bringing Sailfish OS to the Fairphone 4",
        "content": [
          "The goal of this project was to port Sailfish OS to the Fairphone 4, making it possible to run an alternative, privacy-focused mobile operating system on the device. At the time, this was largely unexplored territory, and there was no established Sailfish OS installation for the Fairphone 4 that users could simply install.",
          "Working as part of a student development team, we had to adapt the operating system to the Fairphone's specific hardware. This involved much more than installing an existing operating system: we needed to build the foundations that would allow Sailfish OS to communicate with the phone's hardware and function as a complete mobile operating system."
        ],
        "image": {
          "src": "",
          "alt": "Sailfish OS running on a Fairphone 4"
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "architecture",
        "layout": "image-text",
        "title": "Bridging Two Operating Systems",
        "content": [
          "One of the biggest challenges was that Sailfish OS and Android use different approaches to interacting with hardware. Instead of developing every hardware driver from scratch, we used LineageOS 18.1 as the Android base. This provided the device-specific drivers and hardware components required by the Fairphone 4.",
          "To connect these components to Sailfish OS, we used libhybris, a compatibility layer that allows Linux-based systems to make use of Android libraries and hardware drivers. This created the foundation for running Sailfish OS on top of the Fairphone's existing Android hardware support."
        ],
        "image": {
          "src": "",
          "alt": "Diagram showing the Sailfish OS, libhybris and LineageOS architecture"
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "hardware-adaptation",
        "layout": "full-text",
        "title": "Adapting the Hardware",
        "content": [
          "Getting the operating system to boot was only part of the challenge. We also had to adapt the low-level components responsible for making the Fairphone 4's hardware work correctly. This included building and configuring the Linux kernel, preparing the initial RAM filesystem (initrd), and generating the boot and recovery images required by the device.",
          "We also worked on device-specific configuration and middleware packages. Components such as audio handling, fingerprint support and Android hardware interfaces needed attention to make the operating system usable beyond simply reaching the home screen. This required investigating how the existing Android implementation worked and adapting the relevant components for Sailfish OS."
        ],
        "image": {
          "src": "",
          "alt": "Fairphone 4 hardware adaptation and kernel development"
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "build-system",
        "layout": "text-image",
        "title": "Building an Entire Operating System",
        "content": [
          "The project required setting up a complete cross-compilation and build environment. We worked with two separate development environments: the Sailfish OS Platform SDK for building RPM packages and middleware, and the Hardware Adaptation Build SDK for compiling the Android base, kernel and other low-level components.",
          "We brought these pieces together through a reproducible build process. The resulting packages were assembled into a Sailfish OS image, which could then be installed on the Fairphone 4. We also developed an automated flashing script to simplify the installation process, alongside documentation explaining how to configure the environment, build the system and make further changes."
        ],
        "image": {
          "src": "",
          "alt": "Sailfish OS build environment and flashing process"
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "result",
        "layout": "full-image",
        "title": "A Working Sailfish OS Port",
        "content": [
          "The result was a working Sailfish OS port for the Fairphone 4, based on Sailfish OS 4.5.0.24. The project brought together the device-specific Android components, Linux kernel adaptations, compatibility layers and Sailfish OS packages into an installable system.",
          "The port supported a range of essential phone functions, including Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, GPS, NFC, camera, audio, sensors, fingerprint recognition and telephony. Some limitations remained, such as issues with the earpiece microphone, speaker power handling and eSIM support. Beyond the working installation itself, we left behind the source repositories and build documentation needed for further development and maintenance."
        ],
        "image": {
          "src": "",
          "alt": "Sailfish OS home screen running on the Fairphone 4"
        }
      }
    ],
    "links": [
      {
        "site": "Github",
        "web": "https://github.com/SailfishOS-for-the-fairphone-4",
        "icon": "github-logo"
      }
    ]
